/target
# Any nested build directory, not just the root one. `tools/df-gate` builds DataFusion - 4 GB - and
# a root-anchored pattern let `git add tools/df-gate` try to stage the lot.
**/target/
**/*.rs.bk
.env
.DS_Store

# runtime artefacts
*.redb
abi.json
nuthatch.toml
# …but the committed example nest configs are intentional (no secrets)
!examples/**/nuthatch.toml
# …and so are the OBIB benchmark nests. A published number nobody else can re-run is a claim, not a
# measurement - the whole point of OBIB is that it is reproducible. These files are deliberately
# keyless: the endpoint arrives via `--rpc "$RPC"`, which is OBIB's own methodology.
!obib-case*/nuthatch.toml
# …and the committed tape-companion nests. Same rule as OBIB: keyless, endpoint via `--rpc`.
!docs/bench/nests/**/nuthatch.toml

# guest component build dirs (the staged component .wasm at components/*.wasm IS committed)
components/*/target

# local smoke-test nest (scripts/try-nuthatch.sh)
nuthatch-trial/

# Python bytecode. `git add eval/` swept one of these into 11f898cc - `git add <dir>` sweeps
# exactly as `git add -A` does, and a compiled artifact in the tree is noise at best and a stale
# import at worst.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Scratch from builds, test runs and background jobs, at the repo ROOT only.
#
# **Anchored with a leading slash on purpose.** `docs/bench/*.raw.log` and `tools/**/raw/*.log` are
# tracked measurement artefacts - a bare `*.log` would not untrack them, but it would mean the next
# raw benchmark log needs `git add -f`, and a measurement this project relies on should not be
# harder to commit than the code it measures.
#
# 99 of these had accumulated by 2026-09-01. They are what a careless `git add .` sweeps up, which
# is how a compiled `.pyc` reached main that morning (#1066).
/*.err
/*.out
/*.log

# Local experiment directories that are not artefacts of anything.
/.vercel/
/demo-usdc/
/evaluation-2026-08-23/
/mutants.out.old/
